urce, path, sha256, origin}\". I'll choose:\n       - `\"source\": \"<resolved SPEC.md path>:<line>\"`? Too clever.\n     Let me pick something sensible and self-describing:\n       ```python\n       {\"source\": \"inputs/custody.json: custody_spec\", \"path\": str(spec_md), \"sha256\": ..., \"origin\": \"document\"}\n       ```\n     Hmm, `version_sources`? Simpler: `source` = `str(spec_md)`, `path` = the SPEC.md path too? Redundant.\n\n     Let me look at how the emitter records sources elsewhere: `xp[\"sources\"]` maps field \u2192 `\"file:line\"`. So `source` = file:line naming the SPEC.md line that declares the version would fit nicely: `f\"{spec_md}:{line}\"`. And `path` = the checkout dir as given. That's a coherent choice:\n       - `source`: `\"<SPEC.md path>:<line of the **Version** declaration>\"`\n       - `path`: `str(checkout)` (as given)\n       - `sha256`: digest\n       - `origin`: \"document\"\n\n     Good.\n\n   - git check: \"When a checkout names its own commit (`git -C <dir> rev-parse HEAD` answers) the containing git work tree's HEAD must BE that commit\". Interpretation: run `git -C dir rev-parse HEAD`; if it answers, then the directory is inside a git work tree; the work tree's HEAD is that commit by construction. Hmm, what does it mean then? Perhaps: if the checkout is inside a work tree, the commit it names (its HEAD) must be the work tree's HEAD. But `git -C dir rev-parse HEAD` returns the work tree HEAD already. Maybe the intent is subtree/vendored checkouts that have their own `.git` file? If `dir/.git` doesn't exist, `git -C dir rev-parse HEAD` still answers using the containing tree. So \"the checkout names its own commit\" could mean `dir/.git` exists (its own repo). Then \"the containing git work tree's HEAD must BE that commit\" \u2014 hmm, that'd be for submodules.\n\n     In the test, `sp.document(...)` creates `d = root/\"custody-0.9.9\"`, writes SPEC.md, then `_commit_tree(d, ...)` which runs `git init` in d and commits. So `d`