itter block text is identical in both places, but the following line differs (`xp[\"times\"]`). Include the differing line for uniqueness.\n\nIn build_finish:\n```python\n    xp[\"schema\"] = \"plumbline/x-plumbline/v1\"\n    xp[\"emitter\"] = {...}\n    xp[\"time_tolerance_s\"] = H.TOLERANCE_S\n    xp[\"times\"] = {\"startedAt\": \"packet.json started_at\", \"producedAt\": \"packet.json ended_at\"}\n```\n\nI'll anchor on:\n```python\n    xp[\"schema\"] = \"plumbline/x-plumbline/v1\"\n    xp[\"emitter\"] = {\"name\": \"kit/emit_custody.py\", \"kit_revision\": (rev.get(\"commit\") or \"\")[:7] or None,\n                     \"commit\": rev.get(\"commit\"), \"tree\": rev.get(\"tree\"), \"dirty\": rev.get(\"dirty\"),\n                     \"statedRevision\": rev.get(\"stated\")}\n    xp[\"time_tolerance_s\"] = H.TOLERANCE_S\n    xp[\"times\"] = {\"startedAt\": \"packet.json started_at\", \"producedAt\": \"packet.json ended_at\"}\n```\nunique (the last line). Replace with same plus `xp[\"spec_version\"] = version_block` inserted.\n\nFor `build`:\n```python\n    xp[\"schema\"] = \"plumbline/x-plumbline/v1\"\n    xp[\"hand_times\"] = [hand[k] for k in hand]\n```\nunique. Insert before.\n\nAlso need `version, version_block` computed in both functions.\n\nIn `build_finish`, insert after `rev = H.revision(...)`:\n\n```python\n    rev = H.revision(HERE, job, expect or os.environ.get(\"PLUMBLINE_EXPECT_REVISION\") or None, ref)\n    version, version_block = resolve_spec_version(job, ci or {}, override, ref)\n    rounds = round_records(job)\n```\n\nIn `build`, after `rev = H.revision(...)`:\n\n```python\n    rev = H.revision(HERE, job, expect or os.environ.get(\"PLUMBLINE_EXPECT_REVISION\") or ci.get(\"emitter_revision\"), ref)\n    version, version_block = resolve_spec_version(job, ci, spec_version, ref)\n```\n\nWait: `ci` might be `{}` (when no inputs). `resolve_spec_version(job, {}, ...)` fine.\n\nCareful: in `build`, `ci` is `None` if no inputs then set to `{}` right after. The `rev` line comes after `ci = {}`. Let me check order:\n\n```python\n    have_inputs = inputs_path.is_file()\n    ci = load(inputs_path) if have_inputs else None\n    if ci is None:\n        gaps.add(...)\n        ci = {}\n    job_id = ...\n    xp: dict = ...\n    ...\n    rev = H.revision(...)\n```\n\nYes, `ci` is a dict by then.\n\nNow the signature changes:\n\n`def build_finish(job: Path, packet: dict, gaps: Gaps, ref: H.Refusals, now: datetime, expect: str | None, allow_hand: bool = False) -> tuple[dict, Gaps, list[str]]:`\n\n\u2192 add `, ci: dict | None = None, override: str | None = None`.\n\n`def build(job: Path, inputs_path: Path, ref: H.Refusals | None = None, now: datetime | None = None, expect: str | None = None, allow_hand: bool = False) -> tuple[dict, Gaps, list[str]]:`\n\n\u2192 add `, spec_version: str | None = None`.\n\nNow let me write the resolution function and place it. Best placement: after `ts(...)` / `line_in(...)`? It uses `file_digest` and `H.Refusals`. I'll put it right after `line_in`.\n\nLet me write the final code.\n\n```python\nVERSION_RE = re.compile(r\"^\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+$\")\nSPEC_DECL_RE = re.compile(r\"\\*\\*Version\\s+(\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+)\\b\")\n\n\ndef spec_md_version(spec_md: Path) -> tuple[str | None, int | None]:\n    \"\"\"The version a custody `SPEC.md` declares (its `**Version X.Y.Z \u00b7 ...**` line) and that line's number.\"\"\"\n    try:\n        lines = spec_md.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\", errors=\"replace\").splitlines()\n    except OSError:\n        return None, None\n    for n, ln in enumerate(lines, 1):\n        hit = SPEC_DECL_RE.search(ln)\n        if hit:\n            return hit.group(1), n\n    return None, None\n\n\ndef _git_out(args, cwd: Path) -> str | None:\n    try:\n        return subprocess.run([\"git\", \"-C\", str(cwd), *args], capture_output=True, text=True,\n                              check=True).stdout.strip() or None\n    except (OSError, subprocess.CalledProcessError):\n        return None\n\n\ndef checkout_head(checkout: Path, ref: H.Refusals) -> None:\n    \"\"\"A checkout that names its own commit must be the commit its work tree is at (R2).\"\"\"\n    own = _git_out([\"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\"], checkout)\n    if not own:\n        return\n    top = _git_out([\"rev-parse\", \"--show-toplevel\"], checkout)\n    if not top:\n        return\n    head = _git_out([\"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\"], Path(top))\n    if head and head != own:\n        ref.add(\"spec-version\", f\"the custody checkout {checkout} names commit {own[:12]} but the work tree at \"\n                                f\"{top} is at {head[:12]}: a SPEC.md read beside the wrong commit is not the \"\n                                f\"document the job attests\")\n\n\ndef resolve_spec_version(job: Path, ci: dict | None, override: str | None,\n                         ref: H.Refusals) -> tuple[str | None, dict]:\n    \"\"\"The version this record attests: the custody document the job names, else --spec-version, else a refusal.\n\n    R1: `inputs/custody.json: custody_spec` points at a custody checkout; the record carries the version its\n    SPEC.md declares, digested, with the line that declares it. R3: `--spec-version` is the fallback for a job\n    whose artifact is not a custody tree. R2: with neither, or with a document that cannot be read, the emitter\n    REFUSES (kind `spec-version`) rather than fall back to the flag or to a literal. Both are given and they\n    disagree: refused, because a record can only attest one document.\n    \"\"\"\n    given = (ci or {}).get(\"custody_spec\")\n    doc_version, block = None, {}\n    if given:\n        checkout = Path(str(given)).expanduser()\n        if not checkout.is_absolute():\n            checkout = job / checkout\n        spec_md = checkout / \"SPEC.md\"\n        if not checkout.is_dir():\n            ref.add(\"spec-version\", f\"inputs/custody.json custody_spec {str(given)!r} is not a directory \"\n                                    f\"({checkout}): the document the job attests cannot be read\")\n        elif not spec_md.is_file():\n            ref.add(\"spec-version\", f\"the custody checkout {checkout} carries no SPEC.md: the version the job \"\n                                    f\"attests cannot be read\")\n        else:\n            doc_version, line = spec_md_version(spec_md)\n            block = {\"origin\": \"document\", \"path\": str(checkout),\n                     \"source\": f\"{spec_md}:{line}\" if line else str(spec_md),\n                     \"sha256\": file_digest(spec_md)}\n            if doc_version is None:\n                ref.add(\"spec-version\", f\"{spec_md} declares no `**Version X.Y.Z**`: the version the job attests \"\n                                        f\"cannot be read\")\n            else:\n                checkout_head(checkout, ref)\n    if given and doc_version is None:\n        return None, block                     # a named document that cannot be read never falls back (R2)\n    if override:\n        version = str(override).strip()\n        if not VERSION_RE.match(version):\n            ref.add(\"spec-version\", f\"--spec-version {override!r} is not an X.Y.Z version\")\n            return None, block\n        if doc_version is not None:\n            if version != doc_version:\n                ref.add(\"spec-version\", f\"--spec-version {version!r} disagrees with the document \"\n                                        f\"{block.get('path')}/SPEC.md, which declares {doc_version!r}: the record \"\n                                        f\"can attest only one version, and the emitter will not choose silently\")\n                return None, block\n            return doc_version, block\n        return version, {\"origin\": \"--spec-version\", \"path\": None,\n                         \"source\": \"--spec-version\", \"sha256\": None}\n    if doc_version is not None:\n        return doc_version, block\n    ref.add(\"spec-version\", \"the job names no custody document (inputs/custody.json: custody_spec) and no \"\n                            \"--spec-version was given: predicate.specVersion would have no source, and the \"\n                            \"emitter refuses rather than guess\")\n    return None, {}\n```\n\nWait, order issue: `if given and doc_version is None: return None, block` is placed before the override branch. If `given` is falsy and `doc_version` None \u2192 skip, proceed to override. Good. If `given` truthy and doc bad \u2192 return None with the refusal already added, ignoring the override. Good.\n\nBut careful: if `given` is falsy, `block` = {}. Good.\n\nHmm, one subtle thing: `ref` may already have rows from `H.revision`; returning None still yields exit 3 for those too. Fine.\n\nAnother subtlety: `Path(str(given)).expanduser()` \u2014 if `given` is an absolute path string, fine. If relative, join to job.\n\nNow: `file_digest(spec_md)` reads bytes; if unreadable, raises OSError. We checked `is_file()`. Race aside, fine. But to be safe wrap? `file_digest` is used everywhere unguarded. Fine.\n\nNow, one more important check: the block's `source` for case 1 isn't asserted. Fine.\n\nNow, what about `pred[\"specVersion\"]` when `version` is None (refusal)? Since nothing is written, fine. But `check_order` doesn't care.\n\nHmm, wait: in `build`, the record is returned even when ref is non-empty; `main` checks ref first. Fine.\n\nNow let's write the docstring update for the emitter's module docs? The module docstring lists CLAIMS. It says \"It never hard-codes the level...\" etc. Should we document the version resolution? Good practice: add a paragraph. The brief says don't edit the bar, but the emitter docstring is ours. Let's add a short section describing R1-R3. Keep it concise.\n\nBut careful: `tests/test_emit_spec_version.py` doesn't check docstrings. Add a brief note after the \"WHAT IT WILL NOT DO\" or near the usage line. Let's add to the docstring:\n\n```\nSPEC VERSION. predicate.specVersion is the version the custody document the job attests declares: the\ncheckout named by `inputs/custody.json: custody_spec`, whose SPEC.md `**Version X.Y.Z**` line is read and\nrecorded under x-plumbline.spec_version {source, path, sha256, origin}. A job whose artifact is not a\ncustody tree passes `--spec-version X.Y.Z` (origin \"--spec-version\"). With neither, or with a document that\ncannot be read or declares no version, the emitter REFUSES (kind `spec-version`, exit 3) and writes nothing:\nit never falls back to a literal or to the flag. If both are given and disagree, it refuses rather than pick.\n```\n\nAnd update the usage line:\n```\n    emit_custody.py --job-dir <job> [--inputs inputs/custody.json] [--out FILE | --out-dir DIR] [--print-only]\n                    [--allow-hand-times]\n```\n\u2192 add `[--spec-version X.Y.Z]`.\n\nNow, let's also double check: does anything call `build()` with the `notes` return? `main` unpacks 3 values. Fine.\n\nOne more consideration: the bar's `sp.emit` always passes `--allow-hand-times`, including case 1. With allow_hand, hand times are allowed. In case 1 the document job presumably has hand times; at base with allow-hand-times it exits 0 per docstring. \u2713\n\nNow \u2014 a crucial double-check on case 1: `sp.emit(job, out)` with the document, no override. Our resolution reads `ci[\"custody_spec\"]`. But wait: `add_custody_spec(job, str(checkout))` writes it into `inputs/custody.json` which is `ci`. \u2713 And `inputs_path` default = `job/inputs/custody.json`. \u2713\n\nNow let's double check the round path `build_finish`: does the fixture have `inputs/custody.json` that maybe includes `custody_spec`? Case 2 says \"with no `custody_spec` and no override the emitter must refuse\" \u2014 implying no custody_spec. \u2713\n\nNow, the `build_finish` call in `build` passes `ci`. But `build_finish` also uses `ci` for nothing else. OK.\n\nWait, there's a potential issue: `build_finish` is called before `have_inputs`/`ci` are computed in the o