under `\"key\":`), if any.\"\"\"\n    if value in (None, \"\") or not path.is_file():\n        return None\n    try:\n        lines = path.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\").splitlines()\n    except OSError:\n        return None\n    vs = str(value)\n    hits = [n for n, ln in enumerate(lines, 1) if vs in ln and (key is None or f'\"{key}\"' in ln)]\n    if not hits:\n        hits = [n for n, ln in enumerate(lines, 1) if vs in ln]\n    return hits[0] if hits else None\n```\n\nInsert after this function (before `def load(p: Path):`).\n\nNow, careful about `H.git` vs our `_git_out`. We define our own to catch errors.\n\nWait \u2014 `subprocess` is imported at top (`import argparse, json, os, re, subprocess, sys`). \u2713\n\nNow, one more consideration about `checkout_head`: `git -C <dir> rev-parse HEAD` may emit stderr warnings; capture. Fine.\n\nPotential issue: git may not be configured (`safe.directory`)? In the test env, the bar itself runs `git init` and commits with env overrides. Running `git -C d rev-parse HEAD` as the same user should be fine.\n\nHmm, but there's a subtlety: `_git_out` runs with the current environment, which may not include `GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM`. Fine.\n\nAnother potential issue: in case 4, `custody-collide` checkout committed; fine.\n\nWhat about case 1's `reach_inputs_path` job? No custody_spec.\n\nNow let's reconsider `resolve_spec_version` for case 3's inputs path: `add_custody_spec(job, None)` \u2192 key removed. `ci` has no key \u2192 given None. override \"0.9.7\" \u2192 return \"0.9.7\", block origin \"--spec-version\". \u2713\n\nNow think about `file_digest` import cycle: `resolve_spec_version` defined after `file_digest`? `file_digest` is defined near the top (before `emitter_stamp`). Our helper is inserted after `line_in`, which is after `file_digest`. \u2713\n\nNow, is there any chance the bar's case 2 inputs-job refusal produces a *record* because `main` writes? No.\n\nLet me re-read case 2's third section:\n\n```python\n        exist