ight, let me also think about whether `xp[\"spec_version\"]` might collide with an existing key. No.\n\nNow write all blocks carefully.\n\nLet me get the exact text of the argparse region:\n\n```python\n    ap.add_argument(\"--allow-hand-times\", action=\"store_true\",\n                    help=\"write the record with the hand-entered times listed in x-plumbline.hand_times[] and the \"\n                         \"allowance recorded (default: refuse them, exit 3)\")\n    ap.add_argument(\"--expect-revision\", help=\"the emitter revision (commit or tree, >= 7 hex) this record must \"\n                                              \"be written by; also $PLUMBLINE_EXPECT_REVISION\")\n    a = ap.parse_args(argv)\n```\n\nInsert `--spec-version` between.\n\nAnd:\n\n```python\n    ref = H.Refusals()\n    try:\n        record, gaps, _ = build(job, inputs, ref, expect=a.expect_revision, allow_hand=a.allow_hand_times)\n    except (ValueError, OSError) as exc:\n```\n\nReplace the build call.\n\nNow, the docstring exit-code edit. Exact text:\n\n```\nExit: 0 written with no hard gap \u00b7 1 written, but a field the custody schema requires had no source (named in\nx-plumbline.gaps and printed) \u00b7 2 refused (no packet, unreadable inputs, the job is not at a terminal state) \u00b7\n3 refused because the record would misstate its own history (kit/custody_history.py): a hand-entered time that the\nharness log contradicts, a hand time with no --allow-hand-times, a relay counted as a buyer approval, events out of\norder or dated after the close/emission, an endpoint that is a router or a guess (an aggregator call needs a\nprovider pin declared before the seat's first call, every call must be served by that provider alone, and the\n/api/v1/generation reply must NAME it), an approval by the orchestrating agent or a model, or an emitter tree that\nis dirty or not the stated revision. A routing request that did not ask OpenRouter for the pin alone with no\n```\n\nI'll insert \"a predicate.specVersion with neither a document nor --spec-version behind it,\" after \"an approval by the orchestrating agent or a model, \". Let me craft:\n\nSearch:\n```\n/api/v1/generation reply must NAME it), an approval by the orchestrating agent or a model, or an emitter tree that\nis dirty or not the stated revision. A routing request that did not ask OpenRouter for the pin alone with no\n```\nReplace:\n```\n/api/v1/generation reply must NAME it), an approval by the orchestrating agent or a model, a predicate.specVersion\nwith neither a custody document nor --spec-version behind it, or an emitter tree that is dirty or not the stated\nrevision. A routing request that did not ask OpenRouter for the pin alone with no\n```\n\nGood.\n\nNow let me ensure the helpers' insertion anchor:\n\n```python\ndef load(p: Path):\n    return json.loads(p.read_text(encoding=\"utf-8\"))\n```\n\nInsert before `def load`.\n\nNow writing out.\n\nActually, one more thought on `checkout_head`: `git -C d rev-parse HEAD` where d is a fresh repo \u2014 works. But in case 1, `d` was committed with `GIT_ENV` setting author/committer; rev-parse fine.\n\nBut there's a risk: `git` may print to stderr `fatal: ...` and `CalledProcessError`. We catch. Good.\n\nAnother risk: in the grading environment, the checkout `d` is inside the temp root; `git -C d rev-parse --show-toplevel` returns `d`. \u2713\n\nNow, is there a scenario where `custody_spec` points to a directory inside the *main repo* work tree (not its own repo)? Then `rev-parse HEAD` gives the repo HEAD, `--show-toplevel` gives the repo root, HEAD there equals \u2192 no refusal. \u2713\n\nFine.\n\nLet me write the code blocks now.\n\nDouble-check `resolve_spec_version` returns `(None, block)` when the doc is bad \u2014 but `block` may contain `\"origin\": \"document\"` with sha256 of SPEC.md even when the version parse failed. Since we refuse, nothing written. Fine.\n\nAlso when checkout isn't a dir, `block` stays `{}` (we build block only in the else branch). Good.\n\nNow, `if given and doc_version is None: return None, block` \u2014 when `given` is truthy and the doc is bad. Note we've already added the refusal(s). \u2713\n\nAlso if `given` truthy, doc good, version found, and no override \u2192 `if doc_version is not None: return doc_version, block`. \u2713\n\nNow, what if `given` truthy, doc good, and override also given but equal: returns doc version with origin \"document\". Case 4 only tests disagreement. \u2713\n\nLet me write it.\n\nOne more: the bar's case 4 asserts `self.assertNotEqual(origin, \"document\", ...)` only in the rc==0 branch after `self.assertIn(spec, (DOC_VERSION, OVERRIDE_VERSION))`. We refuse, so skipped. \u2713\n\nWrite it all.\n\nAlso note: `resolve_spec_version` name may be referenced in comments I add to the tool.\n\nFinal check on `build_finish` call from `build`: I pass `ci=ci if isinstance(ci, dict) else None`. But if `have_inputs` and `load` returns a list (JSON array), `isinstance(ci, dict)` false \u2192 None. Fine.\n\nNow writing."}, "reasoning_content": "Let me carefully understand the requirements.\n\nWe need to make the bar green. The bar is `tests/test_emit_spec_version.py` (read-only). We may edit `kit/emit_custody.py` and `tools/check_version_claims.py`.\n\nLet me re-read the key requirements carefully.\n\nR1: RESOLVE FROM THE DOCUMENT. The record's `specVersion` is the version the custody document the job attests declares. The job names the checkout:\n(a) `inputs/custody.json` -> `custody_spec` = path to checkout (absolute, or relative to the job dir); record names the SPEC.md read under `predicate.x-plumbline.spec_version = {source, path, sha256, origin}`.\n(b) failing (a), `--spec-version X.Y.Z` on command line: override, record `origin: \"--spec-version\"`.\n\nR2: REFUSE rather than guess. Neither document nor override -> exit 3, refusal naming `spec-version`, NO record written (existing left as-is). A `custody_spec` that does not exist, is not a directory, has no SPEC.md, or whose SPEC.md declares no `**Version X.Y.Z**` is a REFUSAL, never fallback to override, never fallback to literal. When a checkout names its own commit (`git -C <dir> rev-parse HEAD` answers) the containing git work tree's HEAD must BE that commit.\n\nR3: override stays available.\n\nR4: no published verdict moves.\n\nNow let me examine the test cases in detail to know exactly what is asserted.\n\nCase 1:\n```python\nreach_inputs_path(self.sp, \"doc-reach\")\ncheckout = self.sp.document(\"custody-0.9.9\")\njob = self.sp.job(INPUTS_JOB_REL, \"job-document\")\nadd_custody_spec(job, str(checkout))\nout = self.sp.root / \"document.json\"\nrc, text, record = self.sp.emit(job, out)\nassertEqual(rc, 0, ...)\nassertEqual(spec_of(record), DOC_VERSION)  # 0.9.9\nblock = version_block(record)  # predicate.x-plumbline.spec_version\nspec_sha = sha256(SPEC.md bytes)\nassertEqual(block.get(\"sha256\"), spec_sha)\nassertEqual(block.get(\"origin\"), \"document\")\n```\n\nNote `sp.emit` always passes `--allow-hand-times`. And `reach_inputs_path` calls emit with `--spec-version 0.9.7` and requires rc==0 and record carries a specVersion (when the flag exists). So the first call after adding `--spec-version` must successfully emit. That means the inputs-path job with no `custody_spec` and override 0.9.7 must write a record exit 0. Currently at base with no flag it exits 1 (hard gap? let's check). Actually the current `reach_inputs_path` raises CaseFailure if rc != 0 or spec_of(record) is None, unless argparse rejects the flag (rc==2 with \"unrecognized arguments\" and \"--spec-version\" in text). After we add the flag, the emit must succeed with exit 0. Hmm \u2014 does the inputs fixture currently emit exit 0 when given the override (and allow-hand-times)? Let's think: the fixture is `tests/fixtures/custody/job-rejected`. At base, running with `--allow-hand-times` presumably emits. The bar says case 3 at base exits 2 due to argparse. And case 1 says \"a job that attests a readable document must emit (a clean gap-free record is exit 0; this fixture's record is clean at 8590496)\". So the fixture emits exit 0 with the override at base? Actually at base `--spec-version` doesn't exist so we can't test. But the docstring says \"this fixture's record is clean at 8590496\". Let's trust that.\n\nWait, but `reach_inputs_path` is called from case 1 and case 3 and case 4. It calls `sp.emit(job, out, \"--spec-version\", OVERRIDE_VERSION)`. With the flag existing and the job having `custody_spec` removed, the record must carry a specVersion. If resolution requires either document or override, the override satisfies. Must exit 0. If the fixture otherwise has gaps (hard), exit is 1, not 0 \u2014 then `reach_inputs_path` raises CaseFailure. Hmm, but the bar says at base the fixture's record is clean (\"a clean gap-free record is exit 0; this fixture's record is clean at 8590496\"). So presumably at base `python3 kit/emit_custody.py --job-dir tests/fixtures/custody/job-rejected --out ... --allow-hand-times` exits 0. Let me check: the fixture is named \"job-rejected\". Hmm. But the docs say clean. Actually wait, let me look at the bar text again:\n\nCase 1 docstring: \"REQ C1 \u00b7 baseline 8590496: was RED ... The job attests a custody checkout whose SPEC.md declares **Version 0.9.9**. The record must carry specVersion \"0.9.9\" ... At 8590496 the record carries \"0.9.8\" \u2014 the emitter's literal \u2014 and names no document.\"\n\nAnd the assertion message says \"a job that attests a readable document must emit (a clean gap-free record is exit 0; this fixture's record is clean at 8590496). rc={rc}\".\n\nSo yes, at base with the document (the flag absent, the document present), the fixture is clean and exits 0. Good \u2014 so adding resolution from the document keeps exit 0.\n\nCase 2: round path job `dot-plumbline/jobs/plumbline-cl1-time-record-1`, no override, expects exit 3, text contains \"spec-version\", no record written. Also inputs job with no custody_spec -> exit 3. Also existing record left byte-identical.\n\nImportant: the round fixture is copied out of the tree into scratch; the copy is of the job dir only (shutil.copytree(src, dst)). Then emit is run with `--job-dir` pointing at the copy. The job dir contains `packet.json`, round records, custody/record.custody.json maybe. Note that the round path is reached via `is_loop_finish(packet, job)`.\n\nCase 3: `--spec-version 0.9.7` accepted. Inputs path: rc 0, spec 0.9.7, origin \"--spec-version\". Round path: accepts 0/1/3; if rc != 3, record must exist, carry override, no spec-version gap; and `spec_of(record2) == OVERRIDE_VERSION`, `version_block(record2).get(\"origin\") == \"--spec-version\"`. Note: for the round path, even when rc==3 the assertions on spec_of and version_block run unconditionally! Let's look:\n\n```python\n        self.assertIn(rc2, (0, 1, 3), ...)\n        if rc2 != 3:\n            self.assertIsNotNone(record2, ...)\n            self.assertNotIn(\"spec-version\", str([g.get(\"field\") ...]), ...)\n        self.assertEqual(spec_of(record2), OVERRIDE_VERSION, ...)\n        self.assertEqual(version_block(record2).get(\"origin\"), \"--spec-version\", ...)\n```\n\nSo even if rc2 == 3, record2 must be a record with specVersion == 0.9.7 and origin \"--spec-version\". Wait, but case 2 says a refusal writes no record. Hmm. For the round job with an override, the emitter must NOT refuse on spec-version, so rc2 should not be 3 for that reason. Actually rc2 could be 3 for another reason (approval times? Let's look at the case-3 docstring: \"this fixture's two approvals have no `at` in packet.json, inputs/custody.json or custody-events.jsonl, so the round path exits 1 AT BASE with a record written\"). Exit 1 (hard gap), not 3. So the round path with the override should write a record and exit 1 (hard gap due to approvals), meaning record2 exists with spec 0.9.7. Fine.\n\nBut careful: if our resolution refuses because e.g. approvals... no, approval times produce gaps, not refusals in the finish path. In build_finish, approval times aren't even checked. OK.\n\nHmm, but wait: if rc2 == 3 due to some other refusal in the round path (e.g., revision dirty?), then record2 would be None and the unconditional assertions fail. In the scratch copy, the tree is committed clean, so revision is fine. However, `H.revision(HERE, job, ...)` uses `HERE` = the real `kit/` dir of the running emitter (which is in the scratch tree since emit is run with cwd=scratch tree and script path `kit/emit_custody.py` relative... wait, `cmd = [PY, EMITTER_REL, ...]` with cwd=scratch tree; so `__file__` is `kit/emit_custody.py` resolved relative to cwd \u2192 scratch tree's kit/emit_custody.py. So HERE is the scratch tree's kit dir. Good; the copy is committed clean.\n\nBut the job dir is `job-override-round`, a copy of `dot-plumbline/jobs/plumbline-cl1-time-record-1` in the scratch root (outside the tree). `H.revision(HERE, job, ...)`: it computes top = scratch tree root, and job_rel = relative path of job to top... job is `self.root / \"job-override-round\"` where root is the temp dir containing `tree`. So job is outside the tree; `job.resolve().relative_to(top.resolve())` raises ValueError \u2192 job_rel = None. Then dirty check ignores dot-plumbline/ and job_rel (None). Status of the scratch tree is clean. Fine.\n\nCase 4: collision. Document says 0.9.9 and override 0.9.7. Expects rc in (0,3). If rc==3, no record and text contains \"spec-version\". If rc==0, spec in (DOC_VERSION, OVERRIDE_VERSION), origin != \"document\"; if spec == OVERRIDE_VERSION, \"override\" in origin.lower(). So we can choose: refuse on collision (exit 3, naming spec-version), or follow the document with origin something not \"document\"... wait origin != \"document\" when rc==0. Hmm: \"the record says {spec!r} was read from the document; with an override in force the origin must say which one won\". So if we follow the document while an override was given, origin must NOT be \"document\" \u2014 it must say something like \"document (override ignored)\"? Simplest: refuse the collision with exit 3 naming spec-version. That satisfies case 4: rc==3, record None (we write nothing), text contains \"spec-version\". Note `self.assertIn(rc, (0, 3))` and if 3: `assertIsNone(record)` and \"spec-version\" in text. \n\nBut careful with case 1: `reach_inputs_path` adds no custody_spec, so no collision. And case 1 has document + no override. Fine.\n\nAlso case 3 inputs path: no document (custody_spec removed) + override \u2192 fine.\n\nSo decision: if both a document and an override are present and they disagree \u2192 refuse (exit 3) naming spec-version. If they agree? Also fine to accept. But for simplicity: if both present, and the override equals the document version, accept with origin maybe \"document\"? Case 4 only tests disagreement. But the docstring says \"must either follow the document or be refused naming the collision\". Hmm, \"never a third value, never half-from-each\". Let's implement: if both present: if override == document version \u2192 accept with origin \"document\" plus maybe note; else refuse with exit 3 naming spec-version. Actually safer: always refuse when both are present? Case 4 gives override 0.9.7 and doc 0.9.9 \u2192 refuse. Does any other case supply both? Case 1: no override. Case 3: no document. Case 2: neither. So we can simply refuse whenever both are given. But that could be considered hostile; still, the bar allows it. However, hmm, does anything else in the tree pass both? Not relevant to the bar. But let's be a bit nicer: if both and equal, accept; if both and differ, refuse.\n\nWait, though: R1 says \"(b) failing (a), --spec-version\". That suggests the override only applies when the document is absent. Then with both present, following the document would be per spec, but origin must not be \"document\" per case 4 assertion. Hmm, `self.assertNotEqual(origin, \"document\", ...)`. So if we follow the document, we must record origin as something else, e.g. \"document (override ignored)\" or refuse. Refusing is cleanest.\n\nLet me choose: both present (regardless of equality) \u2192 refuse if they disagree; if equal, accept with origin \"document\" \u2014 no wait, that would violate the case-4 assertion only in the disagreement scenario, which we refuse. Equal case isn't tested. I'll handle: if equal, accept with origin \"document\" (it is genuinely read from the document). That's fine.\n\nHmm, but actually \"never `origin: 'document'` when the override won\" \u2014 if they're equal, the override didn't \"win\" per se. Fine.\n\nNow, case 2's second part: inputs job with no custody_spec and no override \u2192 exit 3, text contains \"spec-version\", no record. So the refusal message must include the literal token \"spec-version\". Our refusal message will be something like:\n\n`3: REFUSED (spec-version): ...`\n\nLet's design the resolution.\n\n### Resolution design\n\nAdd to `kit/emit_custody.py`:\n\n```python\nSPEC_VERSION = \"0.9.8\"   # keep the literal (case 7 (d) plants against it)\n```\n\nWait \u2014 case 7 (d) `plant_emitter_disagreement` requires the emitter file to contain a matchable `SPEC_VERSION = \"x.y.z\"` literal; otherwise the case dies with `die()` (exit 2, an error not red? Actually `die` raises SystemExit(2), which in the test runner... hmm, `plant_emitter_disagreement` calls `die()` which prints ERROR and raises SystemExit(2). Inside a unittest test method, SystemExit is a BaseException \u2014 unittest would treat it as an error? Let's see: `raise SystemExit(2)` inside a test \u2192 unittest catches BaseException? TextTestRunner catches Exception, and in Python 3, `unittest` catches `BaseException`? Let's recall: `unittest.TestCase.run` catches `KeyboardInterrupt` specially: `except KeyboardInterrupt: raise`, but for other exceptions, `except:` \u2192 `_addError`. Actually the code:\n\n```python\n        try:\n            self._callTestMethod(testMethod)\n        except KeyboardInterrupt:\n            raise\n        except SkipTest as exception:\n            ...\n        except _ShouldStop:\n            pass\n        except:\n            # ...\n            self._addError(test, err)\n```\n\nYes, a bare `except:` catches SystemExit and records it as an error. So the bar would report an error \u2192 exit 2 (result.errors). Bad. So we must keep the literal `SPEC_VERSION = \"0.9.8\"` in the emitter file, matching `^(SPEC_VERSION\\s*=\\s*\")(\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+)(\"\\s*)$` (with re.M).\n\nBut wait \u2014 if we keep `SPEC_VERSION = \"0.9.8\"` as the fallback default, and the bar's case 1 requires the document to win... fine, we resolve from the document first.\n\nHmm, but the docstring of the bar says: \"An earlier draft of this bar asserted that `SPEC_VERSION = \"0.9.8\"` was gone ... That case was dropped\". So keeping the literal is explicitly allowed. \n\nNow, case 7: the claims tool must publish a claim about `kit/emit_custody.py` that READS the emitter. At base it reads the `SPEC_VERSION` literal. If we keep the literal, the existing claim 6 still works! And count stays 6. And in the planted copy (literal changed to 0.9.5) the tool reports the claim LIES with both values and exits non-zero. That's exactly the base behavior of claim 6. \n\nSo `tools/check_version_claims.py` may not need any change at all! Let me verify case 7 (d): planted copy has `SPEC_VERSION = \"0.9.5\"`; tool reads it, compares to pin 0.9.8, finds mismatch \u2192 prints `kit/emit_custody.py SPEC_VERSION: claims 'v0.9.5', pin holds 'v0.9.8'`, exit 1. `emitter_claim_verdict(listed)` returns (False, \"the claim ... LIES: ... claims 'v0.9.5' while the pin holds 'v0.9.8'\"), which contains both values and \"LIES\". Count unchanged at 6. \n\nSo the trick: keep `SPEC_VERSION = \"0.9.8\"` in the file as the *default* (used only when no document and no override? No! Case 2 says no document + no override must REFUSE, not fall back to the