"\\*\\*Version\\s+(\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+)\", text)`. Also maybe `**Version X.Y.Z**` without the dot. Fine.\n   - sha256 of SPEC.md bytes.\n   - path: what should it be? R1 says `{source, path, sha256, origin}`. `source` presumably names the file, e.g. \"SPEC.md\" or the full path. Let's set:\n     - `\"source\": \"inputs/custody.json: custody_spec\"`? Hmm. Let me think about what a reader would want. The docstring R1: \"the record names the SPEC.md it read under `predicate.x-plumbline.spec_version = {source, path, sha256, origin}`\". I'll set:\n       - `source`: the SPEC.md path \u2014 e.g. `<checkout>/SPEC.md` as given (absolute) or maybe job-relative if inside job. Let's use the SPEC.md path as a string (absolute if outside).\n       - `path`: the checkout directory (as given).\n       - `sha256`: digest of SPEC.md\n       - `origin`: \"document\"\n     Hmm, `source` vs `path` distinction is ambiguous. Maybe `source` = \"custody_spec\" and `path` = the SPEC.md path? The bar only checks sha256 and origin. The R1 wording \"the record names the SPEC.md it read under ... = {source, path, sha256, origin}\". I'll choose:\n       - `\"source\": \"<resolved SPEC.md path>:<line>\"`? Too clever.\n     Let me pick something sensible and self-describing:\n       ```python\n       {\"source\": \"inputs/custody.json: custody_spec\", \"path\": str(spec_md), \"sha256\": ..., \"origin\": \"document\"}\n       ```\n     Hmm, `version_sources`? Simpler: `source` = `str(spec_md)`, `path` = the SPEC.md path too? Redundant.\n\n     Let me look at how the emitter records sources elsewhere: `xp[\"sources\"]` maps field \u2192 `\"file:line\"`. So `source` = file:line naming the SPEC.md line that declares the version would fit nicely: `f\"{spec_md}:{line}\"`. And `path` = the checkout dir as given. That's a coherent choice:\n       - `source`: `\"<SPEC.md path>:<line of the **Version** declaration>\"`\n       - `path`: `str(checkout)` (as given)\n       - `sha256`: digest\n       - `origin`: \"document\"\n\n     Good.\n\n   - git check: \"When a checkout names its own commit (`git -C <dir> rev-parse HEAD` answers) the containing git work tree's HEAD must BE that commit\". Interpretation: run `git -C dir rev-parse HEAD`; if it answers, then the directory is inside a git work tree; the work tree's HEAD is that commit by construction. Hmm, what does it mean then? Perhaps: if the checkout is inside a work tree, the commit it names (its HEAD) must be the work tree's HEAD. But `git -C dir rev-parse HEAD` returns the work tree HEAD already. Maybe the intent is subtree/vendored checkouts that have their own `.git` file? If `dir/.git` doesn't exist, `git -C dir rev-parse HEAD` still answers using the containing tree. So \"the checkout names its own commit\" could mean `dir/.git` exists (its own repo). Then \"the containing git work tree's HEAD must BE that commit\" \u2014 hmm, that'd be for submodules.\n\n     In the test, `sp.document(...)` creates `d = root/\"custody-0.9.9\"`, writes SPEC.md, then `_commit_tree(d, ...)` which runs `git init` in d and commits. So `d` has its own `.git` and its HEAD. The scratch root `self.root` is not a git repo. So there's no containing work tree beyond `d` itself.\n\n     So the check would be: `git -C d rev-parse HEAD` answers (yes) \u2192 and the \"containing git work tree's HEAD\" is the same (since d is its own toplevel) \u2192 matches. Trivially passes. Unless we interpret it as: if `d` is inside the main repo's work tree and `d/.git` is absent, then HEAD is the main repo's HEAD and the checkout \"names\" that same commit \u2192 passes.\n\n     I think the practical implementation:\n     ```python\n     try:\n         own = H.git([\"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\"], d).strip()\n     except (OSError, subprocess.CalledProcessError):\n         own = None\n     if own:\n         try:\n             top = H.git([\"rev-parse\", \"--show-toplevel\"], d).strip()\n             head = H.git([\"rev-parse\", \"HEAD\"], Path(top)).strip()\n         except ...:\n             refuse\n         if head != own: refuse\n     ```\n     That's trivially equal unless weird. Hmm.\n\n     Alternatively, the intended check (from `custody_history.revision` pattern): if the checkout is *inside* the emitter's own work tree... not our case.\n\n     Honestly, this check is not exercised by the bar (the doc committed its own repo; HEAD equals itself). I'll implement the \"if it answers HEAD, require the containing work tree's HEAD to equal it\" in the straightforward way, and if git fails/unavailable treat as \"names no commit\" (no check). But careful: `H.git` raises `CalledProcessError` (check=True). We must catch. Also `git` may prompt for config; set no env? `H.git` uses subprocess.run without env; fine.\n\n     Also: could this refusal accidentally fire in case 1? d is its own repo at HEAD; toplevel == d; head == own. Fine.\n\n     Wait, in case 1 the bar copies the job and points `custody_spec` at `checkout` (absolute). But `Scratch.document` commits. In case 4 (`custody-collide`), also committed. Fine.\n\n     One more: does `git rev-parse HEAD` in `d` succeed if `d` has no commits? We commit, so yes.\n\n   - digest: `sha256(SPEC.md bytes)`.\n\n9. Now `--spec-version` validation: match `^\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+$`? Case 3 uses \"0.9.7\". If malformed \u2192 refuse (kind spec-version). Fine.\n\n10. What about jobs that *should* still emit without a flag? E.g. other tests (`tests/test_emit_custody.py`) that run the emitter on fixtures without custody_spec and without --spec-version would now get exit 3 instead of a record. That would break other tests in the repo. Hmm. But the bar mandates refusal. The job says \"The failing tests ARE the spec\", and only the two files may be edited; other tests might break, but we're told not to edit them. Hmm, \"Do not widen the editable set\". So if other tests break, that's presumably out of scope (maybe the tree's other tests already call with the flag? unlikely). Let's not worry; the